data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetProperty` value must now be a lambda expression

In EF Core 10, the value argument to `SetProperty` must be an `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>` (a lambda), not a bare `Expression.Constant`. This allows the translator to handle parameterization uniformly.

**Fix:** Wrap the constant in a lambda:

```csharp
var sParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b2");

// Before (broken):
//   Expression.Constant("foo")

// After:
Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
    Expression.Constant("foo"), sParameter)
```

Full corrected snippet:

```csharp
if (nameChanged)
{
    var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
    var valueParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b2");

    setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>>(
        Expression.Call(
            instance: setters.Body,
            methodName: nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty),
            typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
            arguments: new Expression[]
            {
                Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                    Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
                Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                    Expression.Constant("foo"), valueParameter)
            }),
        setters.Parameters);
}
```

---

## 2. Complex-type column naming now includes the navigation name

EF Core 10 prefixes complex-property columns with the navigation name to avoid ambiguity (e.g., `ShippingAddress_Street` and `BillingAddress_Street` instead of two conflicting `Street` columns). Nested complex types stack prefixes (`Payment_BillingAddress_City`).

**Fix — option A:** Generate a new migration and apply it (renames columns):

```bash
dotnet ef migrations add ComplexTypeColumnRename
dotnet ef database update
```

**Fix — option B:** Keep old column names explicitly to avoid schema changes:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, sa =>
    {
        sa.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street");
        // repeat for other properties
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ba =>
    {
        ba.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
    });
});

// Nested:
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, p =>
    {
        p.ComplexProperty(pay => pay.BillingAddress, ba =>
        {
            ba.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("City");
        });
    });
});
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` gains a `CommandSource` parameter

EF Core 10 adds a `CommandSource commandSource` parameter to the diagnostic methods so consumers can distinguish query vs. migration vs. SaveChanges commands.

**Fix:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Diagnostics;

public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // <-- new parameter
    {
        // logging – use commandSource to filter if desired
    }
}
```

Update every method on the interface similarly (`CommandScalarExecuting`, `CommandNonQueryExecuting`, and their `Async`/`Executed` counterparts all gain the same parameter).

---

### Quick checklist

| Area | Symptom | Fix |
|------|---------|-----|
| `SetProperty` expression trees | `InvalidOperationException` at query time | Wrap value in a lambda expression |
| Complex-type columns | Migration diff / `SqlException` column not found | Add migration or pin `HasColumnName` |
| Diagnostics logger | Compile error (missing member) | Add `CommandSource` parameter |
